<Layout title="Trust — Chapter Publishing" description="How Chapter Publishing separates public marketing, authentication, authorship, telemetry, and release evidence."><section class="page-hero shell"><p class="eyebrow">Trust</p><h1>A published chapter should not erase how it came to be.</h1><p>Authorship, review state, account access, operational evidence, and public publication are separate concerns with separate boundaries.</p></section><section class="section shell card-grid"><article><span>Authorship</span><h2>Contribution stays attributable</h2><p>The editorial history is treated as part of the work, not disposable scaffolding.</p></article><article><span>Authentication</span><h2>Credentials stay off this site</h2><p>Signup delegates to the Shared Auth surface; this static Astro site does not collect passwords or session material.</p></article><article><span>Telemetry</span><h2>Operational data stays private</h2><p>Session evidence belongs behind explicit access and retention boundaries, never inside public pages or examples.</p></article></section></Layout>
